arXiv:2510.03833v1 [eess.IV] 04 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shuoyan Wei
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Feng Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shengeng Tang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Runmin Cong
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yao Zhao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Meng Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">and Huihui Bai
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>S. Wei (shuoyan.wei@bjtu.edu.cn), Y. Zhao, and H. Bai are with the Institute of Information Science, Beijing Jiaotong University, Beijing 100044, China, and Beijing Key Laboratory of Advanced Information Science and Network Technology, Beijing 100044, China.
F. Li, S. Tang, and M. Wang are with Hefei University of Technology, Hefei 230009, China.
R. Cong is with Shandong University, Jinan 250100, China.
Corresponding author: Feng Li (fengli@hfut.edu.cn).</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Continuous space-time video super-resolution (C-STVSR) has garnered increasing interest for its capability to reconstruct high-resolution and high-frame-rate videos at arbitrary spatial and temporal scales. However, prevailing methods often generalize poorly, producing unsatisfactory results when applied to out-of-distribution (OOD) scales. To overcome this limitation, we present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>, a novel approach that marries the unique properties of high temporal resolution and high dynamic range encapsulated in event streams to achieve robust and generalizable C-STVSR. Our approach incorporates event-adapted synthesis that capitalizes on the spatiotemporal correlations between frames and events to capture long-term motion trajectories, enabling adaptive interpolation and fusion across space and time. This is then coupled with a local implicit video transformer that integrates local implicit video neural function with cross-scale spatiotemporal attention to learn continuous video representations and generate plausible videos at arbitrary resolutions and frame rates. We further develop <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, which builds a controllable switching mechanism that dynamically determines the reconstruction difficulty for each spatiotemporal pixel based on local event statistics. This allows the model to adaptively route reconstruction along the most suitable pathways at a fine-grained pixel level, substantially reducing computational overhead while maintaining excellent performance. Furthermore, we devise a cross-derivative training strategy that stabilizes the convergence of such a multi-pathway framework through staged cross-optimization. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance on both synthetic and real-world datasets, while maintaining superior generalizability at OOD scales. The code is available at <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/W-Shuoyan/EvEnhancerPlus</span>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Event camera, space-time video super-resolution, implicit neural representation.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video super-resolution (VSR) endeavors to recover high-resolution (HR) videos from their low-resolution (LR) counterparts, which is a fundamental task in low-level computer vision. However, in practice, video resources are mostly archived with both reduced spatial resolution and constrained frame rates (<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, temporal resolution). Consequently, simultaneous restoration of spatial and temporal quality, <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, generating HR and high-frame-rate (HFR) videos, poses significant challenges and remains an essential requirement for a wide range of applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and user experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A naive approach is to cascade separate VSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and video frame interpolation (VFI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> or reverse workflow, which overlooks the correlations between the two sub-tasks, leading to suboptimal exploitation of spatiotemporal information in videos. Some methods have integrated VSR and VFI into a unified framework, <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, space-time video super-resolution (STVSR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which super-resolves the videos with intermediate frame interpolation to increase the spatial and temporal resolution in a single joint stage. Nevertheless, these methods are typically restricted to fixed discrete space and time magnifications, lacking support for arbitrary controls. To overcome this limitation, some researchers study continuous VFI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> or continuous VSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> methods, where the former is capable of variable frame interpolation while the latter enables arbitrary spatial upsampling. They operate in isolation and address only one aspect, either temporal or spatial continuity, failing to provide a holistic solution for continuous reconstruction across both dimensions simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03833v1/Figure1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/147;" width="476" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Comparisons of different C-STVSR methods, including VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, MoTIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, BF-STVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, HR-INR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, our <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> and <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>. (a)-(d) illustrate the different methodology to implement INR, highlighted with a gray background. (e) illustrates the PSNR (dB) comparison for different spatial upsampling scale <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and temporal upsampling scale <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> on GoPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> among these methods.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, continuous STVSR (C-STVSR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> has emerged as a promising approach, which learns video implicit neural representation (INR) to decode 3D spatiotemporal coordinates into RGB values, allowing for the generation of HR and HFR videos arbitrarily. However, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), current attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> mainly decouple the spatiotemporal INR (ST-INR) of video into separate spatial (S-INR) and temporal (T-INR), predicting continuous spatial and temporal feature domains independently. This design fails to fully capitalize on intrinsic spatiotemporal dependencies in the continuous domain, thus hindering reconstruction performance, particularly at scales outside the training distribution. In addition,
these methods approximate intermediate motion fields between consecutive frames captured from conventional frame-based cameras, which proves insufficient for capturing accurate inter-frame temporal features under large or non-linear motions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, motivated by the exceptionally high temporal resolution and high dynamic range characteristics of event cameras, many works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> introduce event data into video restoration tasks to fulfill inter-frame motion fields. Although promising improvements have been achieved, the potential of events in C-STVSR is largely unexplored. HR-INR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> leverages event temporal pyramid representation for dynamic motion, yet it remains constrained by the conventional paradigm of separate INR learning (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In light of the above discussion, we propose an innovative event-based approach <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>, which adeptly integrates the spatiotemporal synergies between video frames and event streams to learn continuous video representations for effective and generalized C-STVSR. Our approach first devises an event-adapted synthesis module (EASM) that executes event-modulated alignment and bidirectional recurrent compensation to explore the event information for long-term holistic motion trajectory modeling. This module incorporates motion cues from input frames with event-based modulation in both forward and backward temporal directions, enabling robust acquisition of latent inter-frame features. We then recurrently propagate the event stream across time and fuse it with aligned frame features in both directions to maximize the gathering of temporal information. In contrast to prior decoupling strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> for video INR, we propose a local implicit video transformer (LIVT) that integrates local implicit video neural function with spatiotemporal attention. LIVT calculates cross-scale 3D attention based on the queried space-time coordinates in the continuous domain and the features derived from EASM within a local sampling grid. In this way, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c), we can efficiently obtain an accurate unified video INR that directly maps any queried continuous spatiotemporal coordinate to its corresponding RGB value, consequently super-resolving videos in arbitrary frames and resolutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, in C-STVSR, an intuitive observation is that the reconstruction difficulty of a pixel is strongly correlated with its spatiotemporal variation. Pixels in regions that exhibit significant motion or fine details often demand more sophisticated processing, while those in static or homogeneous areas can be reconstructed efficiently with simple operations. However, existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> process all pixels uniformly through identical spatial/temporal INRs for upsampling, which causes unnecessary computational overhead. Notably, event streams record the logarithmic intensity changes of each pixel, which provides a straightforward means of assessing such local reconstruction difficulty. To overcome this drawback, we formalize the theoretical relationship between event streams and latent frame changes, further presenting <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(d)). It builds a parameter-free controllable switching mechanism (CSM) that dynamically determines the reconstruction difficulty for each spatiotemporal pixel based on local event statistics, enabling fine-grained adaptive routing of pixels to appropriately complex reconstruction pathways. This not only improves computational efficiency but also allows users to control the trade-offs between performance and efficiency during inference. To effectively integrate multiple pathways within a unified model, a cross-derivative training strategy is developed. We first train derivative networks for individual pathways independently and then integrate them into a single framework, which is finally fine-tuned using cross-initialization with weights inherited from pre-trained derivative networks. This ensures stable convergence and effective cooperation between pathways in <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>. Experiments demonstrate that both <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> and <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> achieve superior effectiveness in arbitrary spatial and temporal scales against existing state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods within training distribution (<span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Dist.</span>). We also validate their preferable
generalizability at out-of-distribution (<span id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span>) scales (<span id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(e)). Furthermore, by leveraging CSM, <span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> reduces computational cost to about 85% of that in <span id="S1.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> while maintaining excellent performance. Main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> that subtly marries the unique advantages of event streams with video frames for C-STVSR. Experiments validate its superior performance over recent SOTA methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose ESAM that enables long-term holistic motion trajectory modeling to acquire informative spatiotemporal features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose LIVT that integrates local implicit video neural function with cross-scale spatiotemporal attention to learn an accurate unified video INR.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further improve <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> with a parameter-free CSM based on pixel-level reconstruction difficulty, <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, optimized by a cross-derivative training strategy, which achieves an optimal trade-off between efficiency and effectiveness.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is an extension of the conference version <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> published in CVPR 2025, referred to as <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>. Compared with the conference version, we significantly extend <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> to <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> in the following aspects: 1) We formalize the theoretical relationship between event streams and latent frame changes. 2) We design the CSM, which is non-parametric and dynamically routes each pixel to computationally appropriate reconstruction pathways based on local event statistics, optimizing both efficiency and effectiveness. 3) We introduce a cross-derivative training strategy that can effectively stabilize the convergence of <span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Super-Resolution (VSR)</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional VSR aims to reconstruct HR videos from LR counterparts, which heavily relies on motion estimation and compensation between frames. Earlier methods address this problem by learning frame-level optical flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Later methods apply deformable convolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> or 3D convolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> to conduct implicit feature-level motion compensation, exhibiting improved effectiveness and efficiency. Some methods are inspired by attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, incorporating spatial-temporal attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, video transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, or state space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> to capture long-range spatiotemporal dependencies. Event cameras are famous as bio-inspired asynchronous sensors with high temporal resolution, high dynamic range, and low latency, which also have advanced VSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. For example, E-VSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> introduces the pioneering event-based VSR method that utilizes event-based asynchronous interpolation to synthesize asynchronous neighboring frames. EvTexture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> regards the voxelized events as high-frequency signals to improve the textures in RGB frames, achieving more effective VSR. Xiao <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> integrate event information in Mamba-based models to capture fine motion details. EGVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> unleashes the potential of events in arbitrary VSR, which learns spatial INR from both modalities but ignores the temporal flexibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Frame Interpolation (VFI)</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VFI involves interpolating latent frames between consecutive video frames to enhance the temporal resolution. Flow-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> leverage optical flow networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> to estimate the bidirectional flows between frames. Kernel-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> utilize local convolution to map inter-frame motion, which synthesizes target pixels by convolving over input frames. In addition, some recent works focus on splatting-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite> or transformer-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>, showing significant advances. As for event-based VFI, TimeLens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and TimeLens++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> innovatively integrate deformation and event-based synthesis processes. TimeLens-XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> utilizes the decomposition of long-term complex motion into multi-step small motions to improve efficiency. CBMNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> utilizes cross-modality information to estimate inter-frame motion fields directly. REFID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, as a flexible method, exhibits robustness even with blurred reference frames. Additionally, several generative methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> exploit the powerful priors of diffusion models to enhance the generalization in real-world data. However, similar to conventional VSR, these methods primarily focus on temporal upsampling independently, where few studies address a unified approach for both.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Space-Time Video Super-Resolution (STVSR)</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">STVSR can be seen as a combination of VSR and VFI, which reconstructs HR videos spatially and temporally. Most existing methods, including traditional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> and deep learning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>, are prone to achieving STVSR with fixed spatial and temporal scales, denoted by F-STVSR. To mitigate the temporal scale constraints, TMNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> proposes controlled frame interpolation with temporal modulation. SAFA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> introduces a scale-adaptive feature aggregation network that iteratively estimates motion with trainable block-wise scale selection. EvSTVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite> combines frame-based optical flow prediction and event-based dense temporal information, coupled with implicit sampling to solve the STVSR task. Indeed, while these methods introduce temporal flexibility to some extent, they remain constrained to fixed or discrete spatial upsampling factors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Neural Representation (INR) in STVSR</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a method of continuous representation of signals, INR is a common means of continuous super-resolution, including image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>, <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> and video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite> tasks. LIIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite> represents the first effort that learns the local implicit image function, enabling continuous extrapolation for arbitrary-scale super-resolution. Inspired by LIIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> pioneers C-STVSR by learning separate spatial and temporal INRs. MoTIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> adds forward spatiotemporal INR to enhance motion field estimation. BF-STVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> applies a B-spline mapper to tackle temporal interpolation and a Fourier mapper to capture spatial frequencies sequentially. HR-INR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> adheres to the same decoupling paradigm as VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, which combines holistic event-frame feature extraction with INR decoding to realize C-STVSR, but requires multiple input frames to improve details, inadvertently introducing error accumulation and computational redundancy. Unlike existing methods, we break the decoupling paradigm. We propose the EASM
that performs event-modulated alignment and bidirectional recurrent compensation to model long-term motion trajectories. Upon this, our LIVT leverages high temporal resolution grids derived from event data to learn a unified spatiotemporal INR, achieving better generalization with reduced model complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03833v1/Figure2A.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/111;" width="476" height="111" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>
The overall backbone of our <span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> and <span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> models consists of an event-adapted synthesis module (EASM), and a local implicit video transformer (LIVT).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Method</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given LR, low-frame-rate (LFR) videos and the corresponding event streams as input, we present an event-based C-STVSR approach named <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> to reconstruct HR and HFR videos on arbitrary spatial and temporal scales. Before elaborating on the components of our methodology, we first provide the details for the event representation in this work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Event Representation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Event cameras asynchronously capture the logarithmic intensity change <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta L</annotation></semantics></math> of each pixel at coordinate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> within the intensity image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta L_{\tau-\Delta\tau\rightarrow\tau}(x,y)=\log I(\tau,x,y)-\log I(\tau-\Delta\tau,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta L_{\tau-\Delta\tau\rightarrow\tau}(x,y)=\log I(\tau,x,y)-\log I(\tau-\Delta\tau,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> in the duration <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau-\Delta\tau,\tau]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau-\Delta\tau,\tau]</annotation></semantics></math> surpasses the contrast threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, an event <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=(\tau,x,y,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=(\tau,x,y,p)</annotation></semantics></math> is triggered at timestamp <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> is the polarity. This process can be described as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\begin{cases}+1,&amp;\Delta L(x,y)&gt;+c,\\
-1,&amp;\Delta L(x,y)&lt;-c.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\begin{cases}+1,&amp;\Delta L(x,y)&gt;+c,\\
-1,&amp;\Delta L(x,y)&lt;-c.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For any timestamps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s},\tau_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s},\tau_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, the logarithmic intensity change <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> between frames <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\tau_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\tau_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\tau_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\tau_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}(x,y)=c\int_{\tau_{s}}^{\tau_{e}}p(\tau,x,y)\,d\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}(x,y)=c\int_{\tau_{s}}^{\tau_{e}}p(\tau,x,y)\,d\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to conventional RGB frames, a raw event stream constituted by large amounts of sparse points is capable of continuously capturing intensity changes with high temporal resolution. However, processing such an unstructured sparse format in deep networks is non-trivial. Following existing event-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, we represent the event stream <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the duration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau_{s},\tau_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau_{s},\tau_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math> into a spatiotemporal voxel grid <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times(M+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times(M+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> linearly, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\times W</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the spatial size, aligned with the RGB frame. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M+1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of time bins. The <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th time bin of the voxel grid acquires the distribution from each event <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{m}=\sum_{r}p_{r}\max(0,1-|(m-1)-\frac{\tau_{r}-\tau_{s}}{\tau_{e}-\tau_{s}}M)|)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>r</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{m}=\sum_{r}p_{r}\max(0,1-|(m-1)-\frac{\tau_{r}-\tau_{s}}{\tau_{e}-\tau_{s}}M)|),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,2,...,(M+1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,2,...,(M+1)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are the polarity and timestamp of the event <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
To mitigate the impact of hot pixels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>, we normalize the voxel grid <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{V}_{m}=\frac{\min(V_{m},\eta)}{\eta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>η</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{V}_{m}=\frac{\min(V_{m},\eta)}{\eta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">98</annotation></semantics></math>-th percentile value in the non-zero values of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. To utilize continuous temporal information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, we combine two consecutive time bins into one segment, resulting in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> segments <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[E_{1},E_{2},...,E_{m},...,E_{M-1},E_{M}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[E_{1},E_{2},...,E_{m},...,E_{M-1},E_{M}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Each segment <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> records the intensity information within a time window nearest to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-D Implicit Neural Representation (INR) in STVSR ‣ II Related Work ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overview</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{\textit{LR}}=\{I_{0}^{\textit{LR}},I_{1}^{\textit{LR}}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{\textit{LR}}=\{I_{0}^{\textit{LR}},I_{1}^{\textit{LR}}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> denote two LR RGB frames and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐄</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the event segments from their corresponding event stream <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We propose <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> that super-resolves <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}^{\textit{LR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}^{\textit{LR}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t+1)</annotation></semantics></math> HR video frames <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{I}}^{\textit{HR}}=\{\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{sH\times sW\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{I}}^{\textit{HR}}=\{\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{sH\times sW\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> with any spatial scale <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and temporal scale <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall network is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-D Implicit Neural Representation (INR) in STVSR ‣ II Related Work ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which consists of an event-adapted synthesis module (EASM) and a local implicit video transformer (LIVT).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Generally, we first extract initial frame features <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}^{\textit{LR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}^{\textit{LR}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}^{\textit{LR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}^{\textit{LR}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we use the same structure to extract the initial features of event segments <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{0\rightarrow 1}^{E}=\{F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}\}^{M}_{m=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{0\rightarrow 1}^{E}=\{F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}\}^{M}_{m=1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering that the latent RGB inter-frame features can be captured in forward and backward directions, we also reverse the time and polarity of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{0\rightarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math> to derive backward event features <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{E}_{0\leftarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{E}_{0\leftarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, EASM models long-term holistic motion trajectories guided by events to generate a spatiotemporal feature sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> with high temporal resolution. Finally, LIVT integrates cross-scale spatiotemporal attention with local implicit function to learn continuous video INR that decodes arbitrary space-time coordinates into RGB values, recovering HR video frames <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{I}}^{\textit{HR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{I}}^{\textit{HR}}</annotation></semantics></math> with HFR.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Event-Adapted Synthesis Module</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As depicted in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C Event-Adapted Synthesis Module ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, EASM contains two steps: 1) Event-modulated alignment (EMA) incorporates motion cues between input frames with event modulation to acquire latent inter-frame features. Based on the event features and reverse ones, we deploy such alignment in parallel to model the motions forward (“<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>”) and backward (“<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>”). 2) Bidirectional recurrent compensation (BRC) propagates the event stream across time and fuses it with acquired features in both directions to maximize the gathering of temporal information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03833v1/Figure2B.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/212;" width="476" height="212"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>
The detail architecture of the event-adapted synthesis module (EASM), which contains two steps: (a) event-modulated alignment, and (b) bidirectional recurrent compensation. “EMB”: event modulation block.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>1 </span>Event-Modulated Alignment</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given input frame features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, our objective is to infer their intermediate frame features according to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> event segment features. Motivated by the proven efficacy of the PCD module in video restoration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, we construct a customized pyramid integrated with event modulation to learn motion offsets and feature alignment progressively. For clarity, we describe the forward alignment process as an illustrative example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for the forward alignment of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th segment, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C Event-Adapted Synthesis Module ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a), we implement a 3-level pyramid by downsampling the frame and forward event features (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{0\rightarrow 1}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{0\rightarrow 1}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>) to 3 different spatial scales <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times\frac{1}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times\frac{1}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> via stride convolutions. At each level <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we first calculate an initial motion vector by a convolutional block on concatenated frame features, which is then fused with the learned offset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}^{(l+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}^{(l+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(l+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(l+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th level to obtain a motion vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{MV}_{m}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{MV}_{m}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> nearest to the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th segment. Then, we design an event modulation block (EMB) <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{(l)}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{(l)}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to generate modulated <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> controlled by the event feature <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to previous PCD-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, our event-based approach can furnish more deterministic motion cues to facilitate frame synthesis, formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}=\mathcal{M}^{(l)}({MV}_{m}^{(l)},F^{E,m(l)}_{0\rightarrow 1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}=\mathcal{M}^{(l)}({MV}_{m}^{(l)},F^{E,m(l)}_{0\rightarrow 1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{E,m(l)}_{0\rightarrow 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{E,m(l)}_{0\rightarrow 1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the downsampled event feature at level <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can learn the offset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{O}_{m}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{O}_{m}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{MV}_{m}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{MV}_{m}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{MV}_{m}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we use the deformable convolutional network (DCN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite> for feature alignment and interpolation. The deformable alignments at different levels are cascaded and aggregated coarse-to-fine for accuracy improvement via the pyramid workflow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since there are <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> event features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}\}^{M}_{m=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F^{E,m}_{0\rightarrow 1}\}^{M}_{m=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, through the forward modulated alignment, we can produce <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate frame features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{f}=\{F^{LR,f}_{m}\}^{M}_{m=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{f}=\{F^{LR,f}_{m}\}^{M}_{m=1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, for the backward modulated alignment, we can obtain <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{b}=\{F^{LR,b}_{m}\}^{M}_{m=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{b}=\{F^{LR,b}_{m}\}^{M}_{m=1}</annotation></semantics></math>. These two are integrated by channel concatenation with a <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> convolution, forming the interpolated features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{*}=\{F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{*}=\{F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the input frame features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{i}\in\{0,\frac{1}{M+1},....,\frac{m}{M+1},...,\frac{M-1}{M+1},\frac{M}{M+1},1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}\in\{0,\frac{1}{M+1},....,\frac{m}{M+1},...,\frac{M-1}{M+1},\frac{M}{M+1},1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is aligned with the input frames and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> event segments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>2 </span>Bidirectional Recurrent Compensation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given that the information encapsulated within each event segment is confined to a narrow time window, after the EMA, we propose bidirectional recurrent compensation (BRC) that iteratively fuses the event and frame features to model the global temporal information inherent in two modalities, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C Event-Adapted Synthesis Module ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b). In each recurrent iteration, the compensation block receives backward and forward event segment features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{E,b}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{E,b}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{E,f}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{E,f}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward hidden state from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{f}_{\tau_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{f}_{\tau_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> the previous timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the backward hidden state <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{b}_{\tau_{i+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{b}_{\tau_{i+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the next timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the aligned frame feature <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}}\in\mathbf{F^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}}\in\mathbf{F^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the recurrent compensation processes <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{b}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{b}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{f}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{f}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> at the timestamp <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{\tau_{i}}^{b},h_{\tau_{i}}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{\tau_{i}}^{b},h_{\tau_{i}}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{R}^{b}(\mathcal{C}^{b}(F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}},F^{E,b}_{m}),h_{\tau_{i+1}}^{b})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{R}^{b}(\mathcal{C}^{b}(F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}},F^{E,b}_{m}),h_{\tau_{i+1}}^{b}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{\tau_{i}}^{f},h_{\tau_{i}}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{\tau_{i}}^{f},h_{\tau_{i}}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{R}^{f}(\mathcal{C}^{f}(F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}},F^{E,f}_{m}),h_{\tau_{i-1}}^{f},o_{\tau_{i}}^{b})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LR</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{R}^{f}(\mathcal{C}^{f}(F^{\textit{LR}}_{\tau_{i}},F^{E,f}_{m}),h_{\tau_{i-1}}^{f},o_{\tau_{i}}^{b}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{\tau_{i}}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{\tau_{i}}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\tau_{i}}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\tau_{i}}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the output and hidden states respectively in the backward direction, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{\tau_{i}}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{\tau_{i}}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\tau_{i}}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\tau_{i}}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the forward. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> calculate the channel attention on event and frame features during the forward and backward recurrent iterations, respectively. At sequence boundaries, if there is no preceding state or aligned event segments, the corresponding computation is skipped. By recurrently propagating the event information across time, we can comprehensively exploit the high temporal resolution properties of events to refine the frame features and maximize the gathering of temporal information. To retain valid information, a bidirectional feature fusion is conducted by the element-wise addition between the input feature fed into BRC and its output bidirectional features as</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{F}}=\mathbf{F^{*}}+\text{BRC}(\mathbf{F^{*}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>BRC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{F}}=\mathbf{F^{*}}+\text{BRC}(\mathbf{F^{*}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Local Implicit Video Transformer</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the EASM, we obtain the discrete latent feature sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The other key in this method is to learn a continuous video INR that specifies the feature at the queried space-time coordinate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is transferred to any target spatial scale <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and temporal scale <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (see the right portion of Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-D Implicit Neural Representation (INR) in STVSR ‣ II Related Work ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Previous C-STVSR methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> learn spatial and temporal INR separately to realize video INR, which fails to fully capitalize on the spatiotemporal dependencies in the continuous domain. In this work, we propose LIVT, which calculates cross-scale 3D attention based on the queried space-time coordinates in the continuous domain over a local sampling grid to learn an accurate unified video INR directly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03833v1/Figure2C.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/355;" width="476" height="355"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>
Structure of the local implicit video transformer (LIVT), which integrates 3D local spatiotemporal attention with implicit neural function to learn continuous video INR to reconstruct HR and HFR video frames.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, with the LR feature sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> from EASM, we encode them into three embedding spaces through 3D convolution: query <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, key <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, we do not calculate the spatiotemporal attention across the overall duration. Assuming the temporal length and spatial size of the local grid in LR scale are <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{G}\times W^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{G}\times W^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. For the full duration <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, to generate a frame at the target timestamp <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we only need to select the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding features nearest to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> along the temporal dimension.
We denote the set of candidate timestamps <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\tau}\subseteq[0,\frac{1}{M+1},....,\frac{M}{M+1},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\tau}\subseteq[0,\frac{1}{M+1},....,\frac{M}{M+1},1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the size of the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert\mathbf{S}_{\tau}\rvert=T^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert\mathbf{S}_{\tau}\rvert=T^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is selected as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{\tau}=\underset{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}{\arg\min}\sum_{\tau_{i}\in\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\lvert\tau_{i}-\tau\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐒</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">τ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{\tau}=\underset{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}{\arg\min}\sum_{\tau_{i}\in\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\lvert\tau_{i}-\tau\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Based on Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we conduct temporal selection on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then reshape them to get <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, which allows the learning for 3D local attention in a sub-duration nearest to the target timestamp <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. On top of this, we employ 3D trilinear sampling on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> to get the query <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{s^{2}HW\times 1\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{s^{2}HW\times 1\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> for a certain frame, where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel numbers. Besides, we employ nearest sampling on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> to get <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. However, since both <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> have the size of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}HW\times T^{G}H^{G}W^{G}\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}HW\times T^{G}H^{G}W^{G}\times C</annotation></semantics></math>, directly computing the attention would incur prohibitively high computational costs. To mitigate this, we propose to unfold the sequence along the temporal dimension and calculate cross-scale attention separately on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings. Thus, we sample from <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> using 2D nearest local sampling at each time slice <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, getting <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Moreover, we encode and reshape the spatiotemporal relative coordinates <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathbf{C}}=\{(\delta\tau,\delta x,\delta y)\}\in(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathbf{C}}=\{(\delta\tau,\delta x,\delta y)\}\in(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> from each query point to all pixel points within its local grid, obtaining the spatiotemporal bias <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\tau_{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{s^{2}HW\times H^{G}W^{G}\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\tau_{i}}\in\mathbb{R}^{s^{2}HW\times H^{G}W^{G}\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cosine positional encoding <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> used here is an extension of  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{G}(\delta_{\mathbf{C}})=[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐂</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{G}(\delta_{\mathbf{C}})=[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sin(2^{0}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),\cos(2^{0}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),...," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sin(2^{0}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),\cos(2^{0}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),...,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sin(2^{L-1}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),\cos(2^{L-1}\delta_{\mathbf{C}})]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐂</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sin(2^{L-1}\delta_{\mathbf{C}}),\cos(2^{L-1}\delta_{\mathbf{C}})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter set to 10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We compute the inner product between <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and add it with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the local attention map at the timestamp <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> after a Softmax operation. After the matrix multiplication with <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can derive the output embedding <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This procedure is described as</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}=\text{Softmax}(\frac{\widehat{Q}_{\tau}\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{C}}+B_{\tau_{i}})\otimes\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><mi>C</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}=\text{Softmax}(\frac{\widehat{Q}_{\tau}\widehat{K}_{\tau_{i}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{C}}+B_{\tau_{i}})\otimes\widehat{V}_{\tau_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We concatenate the output embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{\tau_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> along the channel dimension to construct the complete spatiotemporal INR to yield the features <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Z}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Z}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to space-time coordinates. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, we decode the features into RGB values using a 5-layer MLP with the previous query <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> to reconstruct the target RGB frame <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\mathcal{\tau}}^{\textit{HR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\mathcal{\tau}}^{\textit{HR}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03833v1/Figure3.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>The motivation of CSM. (a) is the ground-truth (GT) frame. (b) and (c) are the residual intensity maps that represent the difference between the GT frame and reconstructed frames by a simple upsampler <math id="S3.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a complex upsampler <math id="S3.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. (d) denotes the quality bias between (b) and (c). We use Eq. (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-E1 Controllable Switch Mechanism ‣ III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) to calculate the reconstruction difficulty of pixels (f) based on corresponding events (e). Based on Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), we can deploy the distributor <math id="S3.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> that derives the distribution map (g) from (f). The result (h) of the Hadamard product “<math id="S3.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigodot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⨀</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigodot</annotation></semantics></math>” between (d) and (g) reflects the reconstruction discrepancy between the remaining regions by <span id="S3.F5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03833v1/Figure4.png" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/99;" width="476" height="99" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>The overall training and inference process of our <span id="S3.F6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>. (a) and (b) show the training stages, which follow the cross-derivative training strategy. (c) shows the inference stage. The light gray background denotes each independent reconstruction network, and the red symbols indicate the weight used for a component during initialization.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">EvEnhancerPlus</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed, INR plays a significant role in C-STVSR to increase the spatial and temporal resolution of input videos. Here, we denote the module learning INR as an upsampler <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> that super-resolves the features <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a pixel <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\tau}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\tau}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> given the spatiotemporal coordinate <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, can be formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}(x,y)=\mathcal{U}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}\text{; }(\tau,x,y))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>; </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}(x,y)=\mathcal{U}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}\text{; }(\tau,x,y)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, pixels in regions characterized by significant motion or intricate details necessitate sophisticated processing, whereas those in static or homogeneous regions can be efficiently reconstructed using simpler operations. Existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> employ a uniform pattern for all pixels, which can result in substantial computational redundancy, particularly for pixels with low reconstruction difficulty. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we construct two upsampler examples, <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, by configuring LIVT with different channel numbers (16 and 64, respectively). It can be observed that for a given HR clean frame <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the simple upsampler <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can achieve comparable reconstruction quality to the complex one <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in certain regions (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b) and <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(c)). The performance gap only arises in regions where <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> struggles, as evidenced by the difference <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\Delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> between the residual error maps <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(d)). This observation suggests that we can design a more efficient approach to selectively upsample pixels without compromising overall reconstruction performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our event-based framework, as formulated in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-A Event Representation ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), there is a theoretical approximate relation between events and latent frame changes, <em id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the event streams inherently capture logarithmic variations per pixel. This property enables direct estimation of the spatiotemporal reconstruction difficulty for pixels using local event statistics. Therefore, we propose <span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, which distributes different pixels to <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> upsamplers <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{U}_{n}(\cdot)\}_{n=0}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{U}_{n}(\cdot)\}_{n=0}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with different computations based on the event statistics, achieving fine-grained adaptive routing of pixels to appropriately complex reconstruction pathways. This can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}(x,y)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\mathcal{U}_{n}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}\text{; }(\tau,x,y))\times\mathcal{D}_{n}(\tau,x,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>; </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}(x,y)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\mathcal{U}_{n}(\hat{\mathbf{F}}\text{; }(\tau,x,y))\times\mathcal{D}_{n}(\tau,x,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the distributor formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}(\tau,x,y)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }R_{\tau}(x,y)\in(\xi_{n},\xi_{n+1}],\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}(\tau,x,y)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }R_{\tau}(x,y)\in(\xi_{n},\xi_{n+1}],\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}(x,y)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}(x,y)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized reconstruction difficulty based on event statistics.
The boundary values of the reconstruction difficulty are defined as <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{N}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{N}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, while the intermediate terms <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N-1}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N-1}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote a set of adjustable trigger thresholds that are arranged in ascending order.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span>1 </span>Controllable Switch Mechanism</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-A Event Representation ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), for any duration <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau_{s},\tau_{e}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau_{s},\tau_{e}]</annotation></semantics></math>, the amount of logarithmic intensity change <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated as</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|=c\ |\int_{\tau_{s}}^{\tau_{e}}p_{r}\,dr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|=c\ |\int_{\tau_{s}}^{\tau_{e}}p_{r}\,dr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(c), we propose the controllable switch mechanism (CSM) that serves as the distributor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{j}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{j}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to derive the reconstruction difficulty <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> from events. First, we employ the max-min normalization on <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|</annotation></semantics></math> to eliminate the effect of the threshold <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, formulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\overline{L}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}=\frac{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|-\min{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}}{\max{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}-\min{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\overline{L}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}=\frac{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|-\min{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}}{\max{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}-\min{|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the lower spatial resolution of event streams than the target RGB frame <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we upsample <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\overline{L}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\overline{L}_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> to align with the spatial size of <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HR</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{\tau}^{\textit{HR}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides, in the upsampling process indicated in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) in our LIVT, an output pixel located at the timestamp <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the latent features at the timestamps <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}\in\mathbf{S}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}\in\mathbf{S}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the intensity change is located at the duration <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}},\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}},\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can obtain the reconstruction difficulty <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\overline{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\overline{L}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}=\text{Up}(\Delta\overline{L}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Up</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}=\text{Up}(\Delta\overline{L}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Up}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Up</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Up}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the spatial upsampling, and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\overline{L}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\overline{L}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-E1 Controllable Switch Mechanism ‣ III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), where the value of <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta L_{\tau_{s}\rightarrow\tau_{e}}|</annotation></semantics></math> becomes equivalent to <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\sum_{r}p_{r}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\sum_{r}p_{r}|</annotation></semantics></math> from events <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\min{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}\rightarrow\max{\mathbf{S}_{\tau}}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is omitted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(e)-<a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(g), assuming that there are two upsamplers (<math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>), the distributor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> using CSM can automatically split pixels into two types of difficulty based on <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way, our <span id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> can achieve a highly competitive performance with the computationally expensive alternative of uniformly processing all pixels with the same complex upsampler (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(h) reveals the minimal reconstruction discrepancy under our method). Moreover, according to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-E1 Controllable Switch Mechanism ‣ III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), CSM is parameter-free, which allows users to flexibly regulate the trade-off between performance and computation by controlling the trigger threshold <math id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> during the inference process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span>2 </span>Cross-Derivative Training Strategy</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As analyzed above, our <span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> involves the implementation of multiple upsamplers. If we train them simultaneously from scratch, the training process would be unstable. To alleviate such instability, we propose a cross-derivative training strategy that stabilizes convergence toward the target model weight <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For clarity, we simplify the overall network <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> into a composite feature extractor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> upsamplers <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{U}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{U}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> ordered from the smallest to largest by their computational complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To be specific, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(a) and <a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(b), there are three stages for training <span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>. In Stage 1, both the temporal scale factor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the spatial scale factor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed. <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is spliced with different <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> deriving <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent networks <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{K}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{K}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the extractor weight <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and upsampler weight <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly initialized and updated during training. In Stage 2, the temporal scale factor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> remains fixed while the spatial scale factor <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> varies uniformly. The pre-trained weights <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> from Stage 1 are used to initialize corresponding networks, which are further fine-tuned under varying spatial scales to obtain <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{F}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{F}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure robust handling of pixels with high reconstruction difficulty, following the order, the weights derived from the most complex upsampler, <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{F}_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{F}_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, are determined as the target weights for the full network <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{U}_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{U}_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. As for Stage 3, the scale factor settings are consistent with those in Stage 2, and the weight of the feature extractor is frozen to <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this configuration, only the remaining upsampler weights are updated based on <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{2}(\mathcal{U}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, producing the optimized weights <math id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{U}_{n})\}_{n=0}^{N-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{W}^{*}(\mathcal{U}_{n})\}_{n=0}^{N-2}</annotation></semantics></math> for each upsampling pathway.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, the original LIVT from <span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> is retained as the most complex upsampler, while the rest are designed to be more lightweight. Therefore, the full model can ensure optimal computation allocation by adaptively routing pixels to different upsamplers. The details of our cross-derivative training strategy are in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-E2 Cross-Derivative Training Strategy ‣ III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Cross-Derivative Training Strategy</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Input:</span><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">EvEnhancerPlus</span><span id="alg1.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> network </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{*}\leftarrow[\mathcal{F};\{\mathcal{U}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒦</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">;</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒰</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{*}\leftarrow[\mathcal{F};\{\mathcal{U}_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">; number of iterations </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">; and temporal scale factor </span><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> and spatial scale factor </span><math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">Stage 1:</span><span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> Fix </span><math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> and </span><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">for</span><span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> to </span><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">do</span><span id="alg1.l3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Duplicate </span><math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{n}\leftarrow\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{n}\leftarrow\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Splice </span><math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> and </span><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒰</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> into a derived network </span><math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{n}\leftarrow[\mathcal{F}_{n};\mathcal{U}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒦</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒰</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{n}\leftarrow[\mathcal{F}_{n};\mathcal{U}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Initialize weights </span><math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐖</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{F}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐖</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒰</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1}(\mathcal{U}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
