<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, we set the number of event segments <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=7</annotation></semantics></math>. In EASM, each convolutional block comprises a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> kernel with 64 channels activated by LeakyReLU. There are two convolutional blocks in all residual blocks. In LIVT, the local grid has the size of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}=3\times 3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}=3\times 3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> and all 3D convolutional layers have the kernel size of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The MLP with a hidden dimension of 256 uses GELU activation. For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>,
Referring to VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, the training for our model comprises two stages. In the first stage, we set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=8</annotation></semantics></math>, training for 450K iterations on Adobe240 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>. In the second stage, we uniformly sample <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,4]</annotation></semantics></math> and fine-tune for another 150K iterations on the same dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, there are two upsamplers with different configurations: one is a lightweight LIVT <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with convolutional layers of 16 channels and the other is the original LIVT <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> (64 channels). In this case, there is an adjustable threshold <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> in CSM. The detailed settings of the cross-derivative training strategy are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>]</cite> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply cosine annealing to decay the learning rate from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math> over 150K iterations. For data augmentation, we randomly crop <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> spatial regions from downsampled images and event voxel grids with random rotations and horizontal flips. The model is optimized with Charbonnier loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite> using <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{2}=1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{2}=1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> with a batch size of 4. The entire training process runs on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs. To measure our models, we calculate the PSNR (dB) and SSIM on the Y channel.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03833v1/Figure5A.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/198;" width="476" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Qualitative comparison for <span id="S4.F7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Dist.</span> scale (<math id="S4.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math>) on the GoPro dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Residual intensity maps corresponding to the same regions in each frame as those in the GT frames.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison with State-of-the-Art Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We comprehensively consider frame-based and event-based one-stage or two-stage state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods for comparison:
1) two-stage event-based STVSR methods consisting of event-based VFI (TimeLens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, REFID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, CBMNet-L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>) and event-based VSR (EGVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, EvTexture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>); 2) one-stage frame-based F-STVSR methods (Zooming Slow-Mo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, TMNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, VideoINR-<em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fixed</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, SAFA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>), and event-based ones (EvSTVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite>);
3) one-stage frame-based C-STVSR methods (VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, MoTIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, BF-STVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>), and event-based ones (HR-INR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>).
For fair comparison as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, all VFI and STVSR models are trained on the same Adobe240 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>, unless otherwise specified. Due to the unavailability of source codes for certain methods, we adopt the officially released pre-trained model of TimeLens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, while the results of EvSTVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite> and HR-INR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> are cited from their original papers.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>1 </span>Quantitative Comparison</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> presents the results for the F-STVSR task, where the temporal scale <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=8</annotation></semantics></math> and the spatial scale <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math> fall within the training distribution (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Dist.</span>). “-<em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Center</em>” represents the average performance of the reconstructed left, right, and center frames. “-<em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Average</em>” represents the average performance of all reconstructed frames. It can be observed that both <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> significantly outperform most existing methods in all settings with much fewer model parameters, which achieve more than 0.5 dB PSNR gains. By using CSM in <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> to adaptively route pixels to different upsamplers, it demonstrates consistent improvements in most cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we also evaluate our models on the C-STVSR task, where spatial and temporal scales are out of the training distribution (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span>). The results are averaged over all reconstructed frames and illustrated in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. Benefiting from the cross-scale attention in LIVT, both our models exceed almost all existing methods at <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span> scales, especially at the challenging large temporal scale, verifying superior effectiveness and generalization ability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>2 </span>Model Complexity</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the model efficiency of the proposed methods, in addition to the parameter comparison in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we compare the TFLOPs with existing C-STVSR methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> on the GoPro dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-B2 Model Complexity ‣ IV-B Comparison with State-of-the-Art Methods ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. We calculate on the upsampling of a clip with a resolution of 180 <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 320 by a spatial upsampling scale <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math> at different temporal upsampling scales <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown, VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> shows the fewest TFLOPs but more parameters and the worst performance (see Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-A1 Datasets ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>). MoTIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> and BF-STVSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> are more computationally efficient than ours at low temporal scales (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>). However, as the temporal scale increases, their computational costs increase and exceed both our models. We can also see that <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> is obviously more computationally efficient than <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>, saving about 12.8% TFLOPs. This can be attributed to the adaptive pathways design in CSM based on the difficulty in pixel reconstruction. The overall comparisons validate that our methods can achieve an optimal trade-off between effectiveness, generalizability, and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE IV: </span>TFLOPs comparisons of C-STVSR models on the GoPro dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> at different temporal upsampling scales <math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (fixed <math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math>). <sup id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> indicates the minimum repeated iterations during inference on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU due to their large computations.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T4.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:66.1pt;vertical-align:-30.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.9pt,3.8pt) scale(0.896757146517389,0.896757146517389) ;">
