</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local Implicit Video Transformer (LIVT).</span> Table <a href="#S4.T10" title="TABLE X ‣ IV-C1 Network Architecture ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">X</span></a> investigates the effect of LIVT design. We first implement a model based on the 2D decoupling strategy from VideoINR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, while incorporating local attention, cell decoding concatenation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, and the previous query concatenation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. This configuration already outperforms the baseline model without these components. When we replace the 2D decoupling with our unified video INR, the model achieves improvements particularly under <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span> conditions. The concatenation of cell decoding and the previous query yields greater performance gains at <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span> scales compared to <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Dist.</span> settings, demonstrating that retaining these components remains effective. Notably, the addition of local attention significantly enhances performance, demonstrating its importance in learning an accurate unified video INR. We further observe that removing the cell decoding concatenation from the original LIVT in <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span> has a negligible impact under <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span> conditions, and even leads to performance improvements under <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Dist.</span> settings, thus we adopt this modification in the extended version.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In LIVT, the local attention is calculated from cross-scale features, <em id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the query is obtained from the large-scale features at the HR grid after trilinear upsampling, while the key and value are obtained from the small-scale at the local LR grids nearest to the query. Our cross-scale attention can be seen as a 3D cross-scale derivation of neighborhood attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite>. As illustrated in Table <a href="#S4.T11" title="TABLE XI ‣ IV-C1 Network Architecture ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XI</span></a>, it exhibits suboptimal performance if we use this neighborhood attention directly on the same feature scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Besides, we encode and reshape the spatiotemporal relative coordinates <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta\tau,\delta x,\delta y)\in(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta\tau,\delta x,\delta y)\in(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> from each query point to all pixel points within its local grid via the cosine positional encoding (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-D Local Implicit Video Transformer ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)). Here, we investigate its impact by comparing it with another learnable positional encoding scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>]</cite>. Table <a href="#S4.T11" title="TABLE XI ‣ IV-C1 Network Architecture ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XI</span></a> shows that the model with cosine positional encoding is superior to the learnable one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter Analysis.</span> Here, we investigate hyperparameters including the number of event segments <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in EASM, the local grid size of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of channels <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in LIVT, leveraged in the original <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancer</span>. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T12" title="TABLE XII ‣ IV-C1 Network Architecture ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XII</span></a>, selecting an insufficient number of event segments, local grid size, or number of channels can lead to a degradation in reconstruction quality. Conversely, an overly large number of each can significantly decrease the model efficiency. Considering the balance between performance and complexity, we finally implement <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> using the settings in the last two rows to compare with other methods in this work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE XII: </span>Impact of hyperparameters including the number of event segments <math id="S4.T12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in EASM, the local grid size of <math id="S4.T12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{G}\times H^{G}\times W^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of channels <math id="S4.T12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in LIVT.</figcaption>
