</figure>
<figure id="S4.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03833v1/Curve2.svg" id="S4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 12: </span>Impact of the adjustable threshold <math id="S4.F12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on the performance and efficiency trade-offs in <span id="S4.F12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, evaluated on the BS-ERGB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> at scale <math id="S4.F12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=4</annotation></semantics></math> using PSNR (dB) and FLOPs reduction ratio (relative to the <span id="S4.F12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span> containing only the complex upsampler).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we also investigate the impact of the number of upsamplers <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> integrated within the CSM, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the granularity allocation according to pixel reconstruction difficulty. As illustrated in Table <a href="#S4.T13" title="TABLE XIII ‣ IV-C2 EvEnhancerPlus ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XIII</span></a>, all multi-upsampler configurations (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) improve efficiency compared to the baseline (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Besides, increasing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> from a binary scheme (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>, simple/complex) to a three-tier setup (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math>, simple/medium/complex with LIVT backbones of 16, 32, and 64 channels, respectively) reduces computational costs but leads to more model parameters. Among these, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best balance, which is used as the default setting in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, there are adjustable thresholds <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi_{n}\}_{n=1}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in CSM, which control pixel distribution (see Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-E EvEnhancerPlus ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)). In <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvEnhancerPlus</span>, for the implementation of two upsamplers, we have <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. To investigate the influence of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ IV-C2 EvEnhancerPlus ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, we gradually change the values of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a PSNR-FLOP curve. One can see that the computational cost decreases with increasing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, since a larger portion of pixels is assigned to the simple upsampler. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> means that all pixels are addressed by a simple upsampler, thus involving minimal computations but at the degradation of reconstruction performance due to the overlook of high-difficulty pixels. Besides, since the reconstruction difficulty <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\tau}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\tau}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> of many pixels is 0, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ IV-C2 EvEnhancerPlus ‣ IV-C Ablation Studies ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, the model with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits the best trade-off. Moreover, due to the parameter-free nature of CSM, the threshold can be adjusted flexibly to further improve the efficiency in resource-constrained scenarios with a marginal performance drop.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T14" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE XIV: </span>Ablation studies for different training strategies on the GoPro dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> at <span id="S4.T14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OOD</span> scales (<math id="S4.T14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=12</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=6</annotation></semantics></math>). “WS” denotes weighted sum, and “CDTS” denotes our cross-derivative training strategy.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T14.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:83pt;vertical-align:-39.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-6.9pt,1.7pt) scale(0.961486114938949,0.961486114938949) ;">
<table id="S4.T14.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T14.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T14.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Training</th>
<th id="S4.T14.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_rr ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T14.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Type</span></th>
<td id="S4.T14.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2">Upsampler <math id="S4.T14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T14.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2">Upsampler <math id="S4.T14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
